		{
			"id": "109874238155797487",
			"parentId": "109874223918028054",
			"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@fvsch/109874238103016502",
			"author": {
				"name": "Florens Verschelde",
				"url": "https://hachyderm.io/@fvsch",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/834/091/035/043/627/original/4367bd08dd68da57.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2023-02-16T11:43:42.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Chrome is a good example of both factors playing a major role. It had a lot of merit for sure. It also captured the market quickly and strongly thanks to a huge marketing push by the number one Web giant. Every major Google property was telling users of not-Chrome to switch to Chrome for years (and are probably still doing it).</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "109885688811971370",
			"parentId": "109870944413762255",
			"url": "https://glasgow.social/@patrick/109885688792465742",
			"author": {
				"name": "Patrick Grey",
